/* =====================================================================
 * HOW THIS MATCHER WORKS -- a map of this file
 *
 * This is a complete, dependency-free matcher for ONE pattern:
 *
 *     ERROR-[0-9]{3,5}: [a-z]+
 *
 * There is no pattern parser here and nothing to configure. The
 * pattern was compiled away ahead of time; what is left is two
 * table-driven scanners and the entry points that drive them.
 *
 * ONE SEARCH, END TO END (rx_search, below):
 *
 *   1. FORWARD SCAN. Walk the subject left to right, one byte per
 *      step, moving between numbered states by table lookup. Every
 *      time the scan lands in an ACCEPTING state, remember the
 *      position: a match could end here. Keep going to the end of the
 *      subject, or until the table says no transition exists. This
 *      finds where the match ENDS.
 *
 *   2. PREFILTER SKIP -- a shortcut inside step 1. While the scan is
 *      still in the start state and has found nothing, no byte except
 *      'E' can begin a match, so the loop calls memchr() to jump
 *      straight to the next 'E' instead of stepping over the bytes in
 *      between. Speed only: it can never change the answer.
 *
 *   3. REVERSE SCAN. Walk backwards from the end found in step 1,
 *      through a SECOND set of tables built from the same pattern read
 *      right to left. The furthest-back accepting position is where
 *      the match STARTS. The second pass exists because the forward
 *      tables record only where a match can end, never where the one
 *      that ended there began.
 *
 * rx_search does all of the above. rx_match and rx_match_caps are thin
 * wrappers over it, rx_next_pos is caller-facing encoding residue, and
 * rx_info is a static description of what this artifact is. Each is
 * documented at its definition.
 *
 * READING THE TABLES. Each scanner uses three arrays with the same
 * shape:
 *
 *   *_byte_class     folds all 256 byte values down to the few classes
 *                    this pattern can actually tell apart
 *   *_next_state     one row per state, one column per class; the cell
 *                    is the state to move to, or -1 for "dead, stop"
 *   *_is_accepting   1 where a match may end in that state
 *
 * Every table below carries a legend naming its states or classes.
 * ===================================================================== */

/* The forward tables have 9 byte classes, so each row of the forward
 * transition table is 9 cells wide; likewise for the reverse tables.
 * Both are pattern-derived constants, not tuning knobs. */
#define RX_FORWARD_CLASSES 9
#define RX_REVERSE_CLASSES 9

/* Both scanners start in state 0. */
#define RX_FORWARD_START 0
#define RX_REVERSE_START 0

/* "No position recorded yet" -- the sentinel for the two variables that
 * hold a match boundary before one has been found. Positions are
 * unsigned, so the impossible value is the all-ones one. */
#define RX_NO_POSITION ((size_t)-1)

